
  Second question: what "easier to change environments" actually means in practice.

  [1mThe fork:[0m who picks the environment, and when. Either a person edits something before shipping, or the page works it
  out at load time. That decision is baked into the module's shape, so it's worth getting right now rather than after
  other code starts importing it.

  - [1mManual constant[0m — [38;5;153msettings.js[39m holds an env map ([38;5;153mdev[39m/[38;5;153mstaging[39m/[38;5;153mprod[39m) plus [38;5;153mconst ENV = "dev"[39m at the top; you edit that
    one line. Buys: dead simple, completely explicit, no hidden magic. Costs: someone has to remember to flip it before
    deploying — this is the classic way a prod page ships pointing at the dev API. Cheap to change later.
  - [1mHostname detection[0m — the module maps [38;5;153mwindow.location.hostname[39m to an environment ([38;5;153mlocalhost[39m → dev, your prod domain →
    prod). Buys: nothing to remember, no way to ship the wrong one. Costs: hostnames get hardcoded in the module, and
    you need to tell me the real domains (or I'll use placeholders you fill in). Cheap to change later.
  - [1mDetection + URL override[0m — hostname detection, but [38;5;153m?env=staging[39m forces one. Buys: point the local page at staging
    without editing code. Costs: more surface than a 30-line webapp needs, and an override reachable by anyone loading
    the page. Genuinely easy to add later if you want it.

  [1mMy recommendation: hostname detection.[0m The stated goal is making environment switching easy, and detection is the only
  one of these where switching costs zero steps and can't be gotten wrong by forgetting. The one thing I need from you
  is the real hostnames — without them I'll write [38;5;153mlocalhost[39m/[38;5;153m127.0.0.1[39m for dev and leave the staging/prod domains as
  marked placeholders in the spec.
